{"arxivId": "1907.11692", "groupId": "abstract-paper-1907.11692", "sourceURL": "https://arxiv.org/abs/1907.11692", "split": "dev", "sampleId": "sample-9283ede295da80ab4c5a", "sourceSampleId": "sample-43195e5364cf989b76e9", "sourceTextSha256": "450f1ca91e1121f8cb78cee9fcab6729692c9b289e1929659b2c201dce8a08c5", "sourceStart": 0, "sourceEnd": 317, "block": 1, "textKind": "abstract_evidence_block", "text": "Language model pretraining has led to significant performance gains but careful comparison between different approaches is challenging. Training is computationally expensive, often done on private datasets of different sizes, and, as we will show, hyperparameter choices have significant impact on the final results. ", "textSha256": "7a1e937bb22ae85c2fc24d3e6b70bf9da939c5777658d33b7c2a6f16a7c6cbdd", "entities": [{"entityId": "e1", "start": 0, "end": 26, "text": "Language model pretraining", "label": "TASK"}], "entityStatus": "complete", "entityReason": ""}
{"arxivId": "1907.11692", "groupId": "abstract-paper-1907.11692", "sourceURL": "https://arxiv.org/abs/1907.11692", "split": "dev", "sampleId": "sample-b08f5adf026f1fb5f31c", "sourceSampleId": "sample-43195e5364cf989b76e9", "sourceTextSha256": "450f1ca91e1121f8cb78cee9fcab6729692c9b289e1929659b2c201dce8a08c5", "sourceStart": 317, "sourceEnd": 602, "block": 2, "textKind": "abstract_evidence_block", "text": "We present a replication study of BERT pretraining (Devlin et al., 2019) that carefully measures the impact of many key hyperparameters and training data size. We find that BERT was significantly undertrained, and can match or exceed the performance of every model published after it. ", "textSha256": "cee2eae9836d1f576d0683ae1b9f981592289ab691e8612128bfa1ed7805e32a", "entities": [{"entityId": "e1", "start": 34, "end": 50, "text": "BERT pretraining", "label": "METHOD"}, {"entityId": "e2", "start": 173, "end": 177, "text": "BERT", "label": "METHOD"}], "entityStatus": "complete", "entityReason": ""}
{"arxivId": "1907.11692", "groupId": "abstract-paper-1907.11692", "sourceURL": "https://arxiv.org/abs/1907.11692", "split": "dev", "sampleId": "sample-1674274798adcbc49e3b", "sourceSampleId": "sample-43195e5364cf989b76e9", "sourceTextSha256": "450f1ca91e1121f8cb78cee9fcab6729692c9b289e1929659b2c201dce8a08c5", "sourceStart": 602, "sourceEnd": 828, "block": 3, "textKind": "abstract_evidence_block", "text": "Our best model achieves state-of-the-art results on GLUE, RACE and SQuAD. These results highlight the importance of previously overlooked design choices, and raise questions about the source of recently reported improvements. ", "textSha256": "6e3e7c45e0df8606729d0040c5e85ca635ad26951a1538cd0c2b06312bd94c28", "entities": [{"entityId": "e1", "start": 52, "end": 56, "text": "GLUE", "label": "DATASET"}, {"entityId": "e2", "start": 58, "end": 62, "text": "RACE", "label": "DATASET"}, {"entityId": "e3", "start": 67, "end": 72, "text": "SQuAD", "label": "DATASET"}], "entityStatus": "complete", "entityReason": ""}
{"arxivId": "1907.11692", "groupId": "abstract-paper-1907.11692", "sourceURL": "https://arxiv.org/abs/1907.11692", "split": "dev", "sampleId": "sample-55ed09bd14b62d7f76c4", "sourceSampleId": "sample-43195e5364cf989b76e9", "sourceTextSha256": "450f1ca91e1121f8cb78cee9fcab6729692c9b289e1929659b2c201dce8a08c5", "sourceStart": 828, "sourceEnd": 859, "block": 4, "textKind": "abstract_evidence_block", "text": "We release our models and code.", "textSha256": "32e8227a21899513d787f331991780f8bcfd2157cec8618ee494a1c4c9a64b40", "entities": [], "entityStatus": "complete", "entityReason": ""}
{"arxivId": "1909.11942", "groupId": "abstract-paper-1909.11942", "sourceURL": "https://arxiv.org/abs/1909.11942", "split": "dev", "sampleId": "sample-b8148d0ce448127bffa0", "sourceSampleId": "sample-74a2479c98c58d01f722", "sourceTextSha256": "3b8a398fd2c0681e9337412e4db33d59ef5975f07d5ae0d7fe74ccbadebc9f34", "sourceStart": 0, "sourceEnd": 253, "block": 1, "textKind": "abstract_evidence_block", "text": "Increasing model size when pretraining natural language representations often results in improved performance on downstream tasks. However, at some point further model increases become harder due to GPU/TPU memory limitations and longer training times. ", "textSha256": "32c5f1ed848daf9d43e5ae31086e8c90464bf39d50b31420fd789995a8d98d7d", "entities": [{"entityId": "e1", "start": 27, "end": 71, "text": "pretraining natural language representations", "label": "TASK"}], "entityStatus": "complete", "entityReason": ""}
{"arxivId": "1909.11942", "groupId": "abstract-paper-1909.11942", "sourceURL": "https://arxiv.org/abs/1909.11942", "split": "dev", "sampleId": "sample-697b5fa87326f84202d2", "sourceSampleId": "sample-74a2479c98c58d01f722", "sourceTextSha256": "3b8a398fd2c0681e9337412e4db33d59ef5975f07d5ae0d7fe74ccbadebc9f34", "sourceStart": 253, "sourceEnd": 529, "block": 2, "textKind": "abstract_evidence_block", "text": "To address these problems, we present two parameter-reduction techniques to lower memory consumption and increase the training speed of BERT. Comprehensive empirical evidence shows that our proposed methods lead to models that scale much better compared to the original BERT. ", "textSha256": "d4904d0bfebb0b2932020e6f8738e6263f8e6c6df032cf1890579283401fe0c2", "entities": [{"entityId": "e1", "start": 42, "end": 72, "text": "parameter-reduction techniques", "label": "METHOD"}, {"entityId": "e2", "start": 76, "end": 100, "text": "lower memory consumption", "label": "TASK"}, {"entityId": "e3", "start": 105, "end": 132, "text": "increase the training speed", "label": "TASK"}, {"entityId": "e4", "start": 136, "end": 140, "text": "BERT", "label": "METHOD"}, {"entityId": "e5", "start": 270, "end": 274, "text": "BERT", "label": "METHOD"}], "entityStatus": "complete", "entityReason": ""}
{"arxivId": "1909.11942", "groupId": "abstract-paper-1909.11942", "sourceURL": "https://arxiv.org/abs/1909.11942", "split": "dev", "sampleId": "sample-65a095ff7f0868d2eb24", "sourceSampleId": "sample-74a2479c98c58d01f722", "sourceTextSha256": "3b8a398fd2c0681e9337412e4db33d59ef5975f07d5ae0d7fe74ccbadebc9f34", "sourceStart": 855, "sourceEnd": 949, "block": 4, "textKind": "abstract_evidence_block", "text": "The code and the pretrained models are available at https://github.com/google-research/ALBERT.", "textSha256": "1051b7f6521abcb1bf5f040fd4212f30e5824a1821c7ecd9cbe1fe84ed6e425f", "entities": [], "entityStatus": "complete", "entityReason": ""}
{"arxivId": "2003.10555", "groupId": "abstract-paper-2003.10555", "sourceURL": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2003.10555", "split": "dev", "sampleId": "sample-6a86d9d62a8b63e66b67", "sourceSampleId": "sample-9fd869fc8bed9bdd4727", "sourceTextSha256": "911ea266bee1c81302e0e0e71c44f852701caaa3349bc915ff589d0d587d9b77", "sourceStart": 0, "sourceEnd": 318, "block": 1, "textKind": "abstract_evidence_block", "text": "Masked language modeling (MLM) pre-training methods such as BERT corrupt the input by replacing some tokens with [MASK] and then train a model to reconstruct the original tokens. While they produce good results when transferred to downstream NLP tasks, they generally require large amounts of compute to be effective. ", "textSha256": "0989c1a2037f57ffa75337fca82accb2231655d189f03c14cdb0969b1e9644a8", "entities": [{"entityId": "e1", "start": 0, "end": 24, "text": "Masked language modeling", "label": "TASK"}, {"entityId": "e2", "start": 26, "end": 29, "text": "MLM", "label": "TASK"}, {"entityId": "e3", "start": 60, "end": 64, "text": "BERT", "label": "METHOD"}, {"entityId": "e4", "start": 146, "end": 177, "text": "reconstruct the original tokens", "label": "TASK"}], "entityStatus": "complete", "entityReason": ""}
{"arxivId": "2003.10555", "groupId": "abstract-paper-2003.10555", "sourceURL": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2003.10555", "split": "dev", "sampleId": "sample-e4448420b82010a0ecd1", "sourceSampleId": "sample-9fd869fc8bed9bdd4727", "sourceTextSha256": "911ea266bee1c81302e0e0e71c44f852701caaa3349bc915ff589d0d587d9b77", "sourceStart": 318, "sourceEnd": 571, "block": 2, "textKind": "abstract_evidence_block", "text": "As an alternative, we propose a more sample-efficient pre-training task called replaced token detection. Instead of masking the input, our approach corrupts it by replacing some tokens with plausible alternatives sampled from a small generator network. ", "textSha256": "62eed9601df860bb590e84ad346a4bd3a0406df60c7e3f41b35fc9c13561c883", "entities": [{"entityId": "e1", "start": 79, "end": 103, "text": "replaced token detection", "label": "TASK"}, {"entityId": "e2", "start": 234, "end": 251, "text": "generator network", "label": "METHOD"}], "entityStatus": "complete", "entityReason": ""}
{"arxivId": "2003.10555", "groupId": "abstract-paper-2003.10555", "sourceURL": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2003.10555", "split": "dev", "sampleId": "sample-31d75271e60d3d1e4ed3", "sourceSampleId": "sample-9fd869fc8bed9bdd4727", "sourceTextSha256": "911ea266bee1c81302e0e0e71c44f852701caaa3349bc915ff589d0d587d9b77", "sourceStart": 571, "sourceEnd": 991, "block": 3, "textKind": "abstract_evidence_block", "text": "Then, instead of training a model that predicts the original identities of the corrupted tokens, we train a discriminative model that predicts whether each token in the corrupted input was replaced by a generator sample or not. Thorough experiments demonstrate this new pre-training task is more efficient than MLM because the task is defined over all input tokens rather than just the small subset that was masked out. ", "textSha256": "c5fe969c22ae94f7e46e91805e6093cdec853d88d162c09743c036c52ec8c798", "entities": [{"entityId": "e1", "start": 39, "end": 95, "text": "predicts the original identities of the corrupted tokens", "label": "TASK"}, {"entityId": "e2", "start": 108, "end": 128, "text": "discriminative model", "label": "METHOD"}, {"entityId": "e3", "start": 134, "end": 226, "text": "predicts whether each token in the corrupted input was replaced by a generator sample or not", "label": "TASK"}, {"entityId": "e4", "start": 311, "end": 314, "text": "MLM", "label": "TASK"}], "entityStatus": "complete", "entityReason": ""}
{"arxivId": "2003.10555", "groupId": "abstract-paper-2003.10555", "sourceURL": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2003.10555", "split": "dev", "sampleId": "sample-458796a8144869d89f22", "sourceSampleId": "sample-9fd869fc8bed9bdd4727", "sourceTextSha256": "911ea266bee1c81302e0e0e71c44f852701caaa3349bc915ff589d0d587d9b77", "sourceStart": 991, "sourceEnd": 1367, "block": 4, "textKind": "abstract_evidence_block", "text": "As a result, the contextual representations learned by our approach substantially outperform the ones learned by BERT given the same model size, data, and compute. The gains are particularly strong for small models; for example, we train a model on one GPU for 4 days that outperforms GPT (trained using 30x more compute) on the GLUE natural language understanding benchmark. ", "textSha256": "ba7f127954b48c140eaa08b2c294644e20b5e267585852986e56a569270d9d8e", "entities": [{"entityId": "e1", "start": 113, "end": 117, "text": "BERT", "label": "METHOD"}, {"entityId": "e2", "start": 285, "end": 288, "text": "GPT", "label": "METHOD"}, {"entityId": "e3", "start": 329, "end": 333, "text": "GLUE", "label": "DATASET"}, {"entityId": "e4", "start": 334, "end": 364, "text": "natural language understanding", "label": "TASK"}], "entityStatus": "complete", "entityReason": ""}
{"arxivId": "2003.10555", "groupId": "abstract-paper-2003.10555", "sourceURL": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2003.10555", "split": "dev", "sampleId": "sample-7c0b78a688c2decc5d9e", "sourceSampleId": "sample-9fd869fc8bed9bdd4727", "sourceTextSha256": "911ea266bee1c81302e0e0e71c44f852701caaa3349bc915ff589d0d587d9b77", "sourceStart": 1367, "sourceEnd": 1558, "block": 5, "textKind": "abstract_evidence_block", "text": "Our approach also works well at scale, where it performs comparably to RoBERTa and XLNet while using less than 1/4 of their compute and outperforms them when using the same amount of compute.", "textSha256": "6cce45a9658425e79e6d1681012702c69d700939464769e29db3bddbd318e17f", "entities": [{"entityId": "e1", "start": 71, "end": 78, "text": "RoBERTa", "label": "METHOD"}, {"entityId": "e2", "start": 83, "end": 88, "text": "XLNet", "label": "METHOD"}], "entityStatus": "complete", "entityReason": ""}
{"arxivId": "2006.03654", "groupId": "abstract-paper-2006.03654", "sourceURL": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.03654", "split": "dev", "sampleId": "sample-652f6a0ff4b78e8eaff9", "sourceSampleId": "sample-fa2a676d1b0eea9d662e", "sourceTextSha256": "6e51be908ca93534b364ac20ace62314008e7b4062020faa0f94c542e346ca3b", "sourceStart": 0, "sourceEnd": 327, "block": 1, "textKind": "abstract_evidence_block", "text": "Recent progress in pre-trained neural language models has significantly improved the performance of many natural language processing (NLP) tasks. In this paper we propose a new model architecture DeBERTa (Decoding-enhanced BERT with disentangled attention) that improves the BERT and RoBERTa models using two novel techniques. ", "textSha256": "8fcee2fe07d4d9e997c22755b057c9dce9601a62ef70d831fb6e495b284a6b91", "entities": [{"entityId": "e1", "start": 19, "end": 53, "text": "pre-trained neural language models", "label": "METHOD"}, {"entityId": "e2", "start": 196, "end": 203, "text": "DeBERTa", "label": "METHOD"}, {"entityId": "e3", "start": 205, "end": 255, "text": "Decoding-enhanced BERT with disentangled attention", "label": "METHOD"}, {"entityId": "e4", "start": 275, "end": 279, "text": "BERT", "label": "METHOD"}, {"entityId": "e5", "start": 284, "end": 291, "text": "RoBERTa", "label": "METHOD"}], "entityStatus": "complete", "entityReason": ""}
{"arxivId": "2006.03654", "groupId": "abstract-paper-2006.03654", "sourceURL": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.03654", "split": "dev", "sampleId": "sample-4a83b428ee47450843d7", "sourceSampleId": "sample-fa2a676d1b0eea9d662e", "sourceTextSha256": "6e51be908ca93534b364ac20ace62314008e7b4062020faa0f94c542e346ca3b", "sourceStart": 327, "sourceEnd": 763, "block": 2, "textKind": "abstract_evidence_block", "text": "The first is the disentangled attention mechanism, where each word is represented using two vectors that encode its content and position, respectively, and the attention weights among words are computed using disentangled matrices on their contents and relative positions, respectively. Second, an enhanced mask decoder is used to incorporate absolute positions in the decoding layer to predict the masked tokens in model pre-training. ", "textSha256": "56a568d12170115b772cbf32a3fbcf1c634966413e46c0c7df3c166fd4b6065b", "entities": [{"entityId": "e1", "start": 17, "end": 49, "text": "disentangled attention mechanism", "label": "METHOD"}, {"entityId": "e2", "start": 298, "end": 319, "text": "enhanced mask decoder", "label": "METHOD"}, {"entityId": "e3", "start": 387, "end": 412, "text": "predict the masked tokens", "label": "TASK"}, {"entityId": "e4", "start": 416, "end": 434, "text": "model pre-training", "label": "TASK"}], "entityStatus": "complete", "entityReason": ""}
{"arxivId": "2006.03654", "groupId": "abstract-paper-2006.03654", "sourceURL": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.03654", "split": "dev", "sampleId": "sample-831cc50e9ca548acb16f", "sourceSampleId": "sample-fa2a676d1b0eea9d662e", "sourceTextSha256": "6e51be908ca93534b364ac20ace62314008e7b4062020faa0f94c542e346ca3b", "sourceStart": 1087, "sourceEnd": 1487, "block": 4, "textKind": "abstract_evidence_block", "text": "Compared to RoBERTa-Large, a DeBERTa model trained on half of the training data performs consistently better on a wide range of NLP tasks, achieving improvements on MNLI by +0.9% (90.2% vs. 91.1%), on SQuAD v2.0 by +2.3% (88.4% vs. 90.7%) and RACE by +3.6% (83.2% vs. 86.8%). Notably, we scale up DeBERTa by training a larger version that consists of 48 Transform layers with 1.5 billion parameters. ", "textSha256": "2678992e728b16efe371c616e7ed0b622831969804535fabeada933671d1b7d4", "entities": [{"entityId": "e1", "start": 12, "end": 25, "text": "RoBERTa-Large", "label": "METHOD"}, {"entityId": "e2", "start": 29, "end": 36, "text": "DeBERTa", "label": "METHOD"}, {"entityId": "e3", "start": 165, "end": 169, "text": "MNLI", "label": "DATASET"}, {"entityId": "e4", "start": 201, "end": 211, "text": "SQuAD v2.0", "label": "DATASET"}, {"entityId": "e5", "start": 243, "end": 247, "text": "RACE", "label": "DATASET"}, {"entityId": "e6", "start": 297, "end": 304, "text": "DeBERTa", "label": "METHOD"}], "entityStatus": "complete", "entityReason": ""}
{"arxivId": "2006.03654", "groupId": "abstract-paper-2006.03654", "sourceURL": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.03654", "split": "dev", "sampleId": "sample-cd09ccbf3060833cfd91", "sourceSampleId": "sample-fa2a676d1b0eea9d662e", "sourceTextSha256": "6e51be908ca93534b364ac20ace62314008e7b4062020faa0f94c542e346ca3b", "sourceStart": 1487, "sourceEnd": 1863, "block": 5, "textKind": "abstract_evidence_block", "text": "The significant performance boost makes the single DeBERTa model surpass the human performance on the SuperGLUE benchmark (Wang et al., 2019a) for the first time in terms of macro-average score (89.9 versus 89.8), and the ensemble DeBERTa model sits atop the SuperGLUE leaderboard as of January 6, 2021, out performing the human baseline by a decent margin (90.3 versus 89.8).", "textSha256": "f79032aaaef1513a8009eb8081ee7945a7d87ecbbe57d858875ede989f0dddee", "entities": [{"entityId": "e1", "start": 51, "end": 58, "text": "DeBERTa", "label": "METHOD"}, {"entityId": "e2", "start": 102, "end": 111, "text": "SuperGLUE", "label": "DATASET"}, {"entityId": "e3", "start": 231, "end": 238, "text": "DeBERTa", "label": "METHOD"}, {"entityId": "e4", "start": 259, "end": 268, "text": "SuperGLUE", "label": "DATASET"}], "entityStatus": "complete", "entityReason": ""}
{"arxivId": "1906.08237", "groupId": "abstract-paper-1906.08237", "sourceURL": "https://arxiv.org/abs/1906.08237", "split": "dev", "sampleId": "sample-90c74532a04ce4229be0", "sourceSampleId": "sample-bda08746d2a6881f1dc2", "sourceTextSha256": "c4ead88e2e9a7ebcd945268ae6494b3522a89b62e48fa235b29625a707ebd05c", "sourceStart": 0, "sourceEnd": 363, "block": 1, "textKind": "abstract_evidence_block", "text": "With the capability of modeling bidirectional contexts, denoising autoencoding based pretraining like BERT achieves better performance than pretraining approaches based on autoregressive language modeling. However, relying on corrupting the input with masks, BERT neglects dependency between the masked positions and suffers from a pretrain-finetune discrepancy. ", "textSha256": "a09e4b51f51c76827b21afb2c9feaad76cfa0f521f4fc684930ef18b85a115ce", "entities": [{"entityId": "e1", "start": 23, "end": 54, "text": "modeling bidirectional contexts", "label": "TASK"}, {"entityId": "e2", "start": 56, "end": 78, "text": "denoising autoencoding", "label": "METHOD"}, {"entityId": "e3", "start": 102, "end": 106, "text": "BERT", "label": "METHOD"}, {"entityId": "e4", "start": 172, "end": 204, "text": "autoregressive language modeling", "label": "METHOD"}, {"entityId": "e5", "start": 259, "end": 263, "text": "BERT", "label": "METHOD"}], "entityStatus": "complete", "entityReason": ""}
{"arxivId": "1906.08237", "groupId": "abstract-paper-1906.08237", "sourceURL": "https://arxiv.org/abs/1906.08237", "split": "dev", "sampleId": "sample-8d5f42e5069650f6e277", "sourceSampleId": "sample-bda08746d2a6881f1dc2", "sourceTextSha256": "c4ead88e2e9a7ebcd945268ae6494b3522a89b62e48fa235b29625a707ebd05c", "sourceStart": 363, "sourceEnd": 800, "block": 2, "textKind": "abstract_evidence_block", "text": "In light of these pros and cons, we propose XLNet, a generalized autoregressive pretraining method that (1) enables learning bidirectional contexts by maximizing the expected likelihood over all permutations of the factorization order and (2) overcomes the limitations of BERT thanks to its autoregressive formulation. Furthermore, XLNet integrates ideas from Transformer-XL, the state-of-the-art autoregressive model, into pretraining. ", "textSha256": "2b56217535695325a6370023b57780158cb258a851893d75f08929637b402135", "entities": [{"entityId": "e1", "start": 44, "end": 49, "text": "XLNet", "label": "METHOD"}, {"entityId": "e2", "start": 53, "end": 91, "text": "generalized autoregressive pretraining", "label": "METHOD"}, {"entityId": "e3", "start": 116, "end": 147, "text": "learning bidirectional contexts", "label": "TASK"}, {"entityId": "e4", "start": 272, "end": 276, "text": "BERT", "label": "METHOD"}, {"entityId": "e5", "start": 332, "end": 337, "text": "XLNet", "label": "METHOD"}, {"entityId": "e6", "start": 360, "end": 374, "text": "Transformer-XL", "label": "METHOD"}], "entityStatus": "complete", "entityReason": ""}
{"arxivId": "1906.08237", "groupId": "abstract-paper-1906.08237", "sourceURL": "https://arxiv.org/abs/1906.08237", "split": "dev", "sampleId": "sample-7b3d278313fc3f91ca6b", "sourceSampleId": "sample-bda08746d2a6881f1dc2", "sourceTextSha256": "c4ead88e2e9a7ebcd945268ae6494b3522a89b62e48fa235b29625a707ebd05c", "sourceStart": 800, "sourceEnd": 1011, "block": 3, "textKind": "abstract_evidence_block", "text": "Empirically, under comparable experiment settings, XLNet outperforms BERT on 20 tasks, often by a large margin, including question answering, natural language inference, sentiment analysis, and document ranking.", "textSha256": "d58cbf6a9e92cf9b0c6213d8f8a9df13d8a1ffdb53f71c9d72311f15f46bde5b", "entities": [{"entityId": "e1", "start": 51, "end": 56, "text": "XLNet", "label": "METHOD"}, {"entityId": "e2", "start": 69, "end": 73, "text": "BERT", "label": "METHOD"}, {"entityId": "e3", "start": 122, "end": 140, "text": "question answering", "label": "TASK"}, {"entityId": "e4", "start": 142, "end": 168, "text": "natural language inference", "label": "TASK"}, {"entityId": "e5", "start": 170, "end": 188, "text": "sentiment analysis", "label": "TASK"}, {"entityId": "e6", "start": 194, "end": 210, "text": "document ranking", "label": "TASK"}], "entityStatus": "complete", "entityReason": ""}
{"arxivId": "1910.10683", "groupId": "abstract-paper-1910.10683", "sourceURL": "https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.10683", "split": "dev", "sampleId": "sample-d958c8bc6c309ca2aa04", "sourceSampleId": "sample-d7bf2d70f6eb322311e4", "sourceTextSha256": "21e3cc6cee4ea8d280be18eb3708378f7ddea24609df0362fb86a94143b6438c", "sourceStart": 0, "sourceEnd": 305, "block": 1, "textKind": "abstract_evidence_block", "text": "Transfer learning, where a model is first pre-trained on a data-rich task before being fine-tuned on a downstream task, has emerged as a powerful technique in natural language processing (NLP). The effectiveness of transfer learning has given rise to a diversity of approaches, methodology, and practice. ", "textSha256": "91dda63e58b9d5025160f55c6621af090917cfc35c630e65d7ea44b4c2319814", "entities": [{"entityId": "e1", "start": 0, "end": 17, "text": "Transfer learning", "label": "METHOD"}, {"entityId": "e2", "start": 215, "end": 232, "text": "transfer learning", "label": "METHOD"}], "entityStatus": "complete", "entityReason": ""}
{"arxivId": "1910.10683", "groupId": "abstract-paper-1910.10683", "sourceURL": "https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.10683", "split": "dev", "sampleId": "sample-806cd90c01f00ed2d411", "sourceSampleId": "sample-d7bf2d70f6eb322311e4", "sourceTextSha256": "21e3cc6cee4ea8d280be18eb3708378f7ddea24609df0362fb86a94143b6438c", "sourceStart": 305, "sourceEnd": 668, "block": 2, "textKind": "abstract_evidence_block", "text": "In this paper, we explore the landscape of transfer learning techniques for NLP by introducing a unified framework that converts all text-based language problems into a text-to-text format. Our systematic study compares pre-training objectives, architectures, unlabeled data sets, transfer approaches, and other factors on dozens of language understanding tasks. ", "textSha256": "05c39ddde8ac2e6eaf8edbe65425f44abe9f05c1cd49fff38255ec3aada08e64", "entities": [{"entityId": "e1", "start": 43, "end": 71, "text": "transfer learning techniques", "label": "METHOD"}, {"entityId": "e2", "start": 97, "end": 114, "text": "unified framework", "label": "METHOD"}, {"entityId": "e3", "start": 120, "end": 188, "text": "converts all text-based language problems into a text-to-text format", "label": "TASK"}, {"entityId": "e4", "start": 333, "end": 355, "text": "language understanding", "label": "TASK"}], "entityStatus": "complete", "entityReason": ""}
{"arxivId": "1910.10683", "groupId": "abstract-paper-1910.10683", "sourceURL": "https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.10683", "split": "dev", "sampleId": "sample-92d81217e2a831abc1b1", "sourceSampleId": "sample-d7bf2d70f6eb322311e4", "sourceTextSha256": "21e3cc6cee4ea8d280be18eb3708378f7ddea24609df0362fb86a94143b6438c", "sourceStart": 668, "sourceEnd": 1013, "block": 3, "textKind": "abstract_evidence_block", "text": "By combining the insights from our exploration with scale and our new ``Colossal Clean Crawled Corpus'', we achieve state-of-the-art results on many benchmarks covering summarization, question answering, text classification, and more. To facilitate future work on transfer learning for NLP, we release our data set, pre-trained models, and code.", "textSha256": "bd18932810ff7f33cc09bae33c7a521283500513f5afa4d2cf92c257d4678bb1", "entities": [{"entityId": "e1", "start": 72, "end": 101, "text": "Colossal Clean Crawled Corpus", "label": "DATASET"}, {"entityId": "e2", "start": 169, "end": 182, "text": "summarization", "label": "TASK"}, {"entityId": "e3", "start": 184, "end": 202, "text": "question answering", "label": "TASK"}, {"entityId": "e4", "start": 204, "end": 223, "text": "text classification", "label": "TASK"}, {"entityId": "e5", "start": 264, "end": 281, "text": "transfer learning", "label": "METHOD"}], "entityStatus": "complete", "entityReason": ""}
{"arxivId": "1910.13461", "groupId": "abstract-paper-1910.13461", "sourceURL": "https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.13461", "split": "dev", "sampleId": "sample-71d1f8359a9fd6b4d037", "sourceSampleId": "sample-27a975883e32b985da39", "sourceTextSha256": "c7934ba3f967f11b47af6799ce3fa9fabb6eabb6e8f49e0bd2b87f8e361e15d2", "sourceStart": 0, "sourceEnd": 220, "block": 1, "textKind": "abstract_evidence_block", "text": "We present BART, a denoising autoencoder for pretraining sequence-to-sequence models. BART is trained by (1) corrupting text with an arbitrary noising function, and (2) learning a model to reconstruct the original text. ", "textSha256": "17160514eb92d1d7a354e6e8ec1c2a012aa314e9667e6f5d237dce4e3380bd33", "entities": [{"entityId": "e1", "start": 11, "end": 15, "text": "BART", "label": "METHOD"}, {"entityId": "e2", "start": 19, "end": 40, "text": "denoising autoencoder", "label": "METHOD"}, {"entityId": "e3", "start": 45, "end": 84, "text": "pretraining sequence-to-sequence models", "label": "TASK"}, {"entityId": "e4", "start": 86, "end": 90, "text": "BART", "label": "METHOD"}, {"entityId": "e5", "start": 189, "end": 218, "text": "reconstruct the original text", "label": "TASK"}], "entityStatus": "complete", "entityReason": ""}
{"arxivId": "1910.13461", "groupId": "abstract-paper-1910.13461", "sourceURL": "https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.13461", "split": "dev", "sampleId": "sample-221c7878daa9bd751263", "sourceSampleId": "sample-27a975883e32b985da39", "sourceTextSha256": "c7934ba3f967f11b47af6799ce3fa9fabb6eabb6e8f49e0bd2b87f8e361e15d2", "sourceStart": 220, "sourceEnd": 714, "block": 2, "textKind": "abstract_evidence_block", "text": "It uses a standard Tranformer-based neural machine translation architecture which, despite its simplicity, can be seen as generalizing BERT (due to the bidirectional encoder), GPT (with the left-to-right decoder), and many other more recent pretraining schemes. We evaluate a number of noising approaches, finding the best performance by both randomly shuffling the order of the original sentences and using a novel in-filling scheme, where spans of text are replaced with a single mask token. ", "textSha256": "7797c3ad0ce19cc2afa1f89f75c199562e6d3b11041a70858e8faa3521d38503", "entities": [{"entityId": "e1", "start": 19, "end": 75, "text": "Tranformer-based neural machine translation architecture", "label": "METHOD"}, {"entityId": "e2", "start": 135, "end": 139, "text": "BERT", "label": "METHOD"}, {"entityId": "e3", "start": 176, "end": 179, "text": "GPT", "label": "METHOD"}, {"entityId": "e4", "start": 416, "end": 433, "text": "in-filling scheme", "label": "METHOD"}], "entityStatus": "complete", "entityReason": ""}
{"arxivId": "1910.13461", "groupId": "abstract-paper-1910.13461", "sourceURL": "https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.13461", "split": "dev", "sampleId": "sample-07ac69dd08b50d96b07b", "sourceSampleId": "sample-27a975883e32b985da39", "sourceTextSha256": "c7934ba3f967f11b47af6799ce3fa9fabb6eabb6e8f49e0bd2b87f8e361e15d2", "sourceStart": 714, "sourceEnd": 1066, "block": 3, "textKind": "abstract_evidence_block", "text": "BART is particularly effective when fine tuned for text generation but also works well for comprehension tasks. It matches the performance of RoBERTa with comparable training resources on GLUE and SQuAD, achieves new state-of-the-art results on a range of abstractive dialogue, question answering, and summarization tasks, with gains of up to 6 ROUGE. ", "textSha256": "fa25a904d85cecc62cbee4392cc1fad1e28973c7a81f743630c73d8676906bfd", "entities": [{"entityId": "e1", "start": 0, "end": 4, "text": "BART", "label": "METHOD"}, {"entityId": "e2", "start": 51, "end": 66, "text": "text generation", "label": "TASK"}, {"entityId": "e3", "start": 91, "end": 110, "text": "comprehension tasks", "label": "TASK"}, {"entityId": "e4", "start": 142, "end": 149, "text": "RoBERTa", "label": "METHOD"}, {"entityId": "e5", "start": 188, "end": 192, "text": "GLUE", "label": "DATASET"}, {"entityId": "e6", "start": 197, "end": 202, "text": "SQuAD", "label": "DATASET"}, {"entityId": "e7", "start": 256, "end": 276, "text": "abstractive dialogue", "label": "TASK"}, {"entityId": "e8", "start": 278, "end": 296, "text": "question answering", "label": "TASK"}, {"entityId": "e9", "start": 302, "end": 315, "text": "summarization", "label": "TASK"}], "entityStatus": "complete", "entityReason": ""}
{"arxivId": "1910.13461", "groupId": "abstract-paper-1910.13461", "sourceURL": "https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.13461", "split": "dev", "sampleId": "sample-fc2003dfe220cc7c600c", "sourceSampleId": "sample-27a975883e32b985da39", "sourceTextSha256": "c7934ba3f967f11b47af6799ce3fa9fabb6eabb6e8f49e0bd2b87f8e361e15d2", "sourceStart": 1066, "sourceEnd": 1372, "block": 4, "textKind": "abstract_evidence_block", "text": "BART also provides a 1.1 BLEU increase over a back-translation system for machine translation, with only target language pretraining. We also report ablation experiments that replicate other pretraining schemes within the BART framework, to better measure which factors most influence end-task performance.", "textSha256": "e549d9a74576b565129ad748be4db3c2e6e54bfd12f22fa1a1f6980ae7fee2f1", "entities": [{"entityId": "e1", "start": 0, "end": 4, "text": "BART", "label": "METHOD"}, {"entityId": "e2", "start": 46, "end": 69, "text": "back-translation system", "label": "METHOD"}, {"entityId": "e3", "start": 74, "end": 93, "text": "machine translation", "label": "TASK"}, {"entityId": "e4", "start": 222, "end": 226, "text": "BART", "label": "METHOD"}], "entityStatus": "complete", "entityReason": ""}
{"arxivId": "1908.10084", "groupId": "abstract-paper-1908.10084", "sourceURL": "https://arxiv.org/abs/1908.10084", "split": "dev", "sampleId": "sample-402ca21955dffe7741c5", "sourceSampleId": "sample-d972dc374490084c3c52", "sourceTextSha256": "0c1c8ed261f75c76beed4bda3912d293019349c6808e27aafbade2829bfd0065", "sourceStart": 803, "sourceEnd": 1120, "block": 3, "textKind": "abstract_evidence_block", "text": "This reduces the effort for finding the most similar pair from 65 hours with BERT / RoBERTa to about 5 seconds with SBERT, while maintaining the accuracy from BERT. We evaluate SBERT and SRoBERTa on common STS tasks and transfer learning tasks, where it outperforms other state-of-the-art sentence embeddings methods.", "textSha256": "f04dd96dd8866a62313ee824def070bea62b9b3dfa92e3ab6489dc3e80f05471", "entities": [{"entityId": "e1", "start": 28, "end": 57, "text": "finding the most similar pair", "label": "TASK"}, {"entityId": "e2", "start": 77, "end": 81, "text": "BERT", "label": "METHOD"}, {"entityId": "e3", "start": 84, "end": 91, "text": "RoBERTa", "label": "METHOD"}, {"entityId": "e4", "start": 116, "end": 121, "text": "SBERT", "label": "METHOD"}, {"entityId": "e5", "start": 159, "end": 163, "text": "BERT", "label": "METHOD"}, {"entityId": "e6", "start": 177, "end": 182, "text": "SBERT", "label": "METHOD"}, {"entityId": "e7", "start": 187, "end": 195, "text": "SRoBERTa", "label": "METHOD"}, {"entityId": "e8", "start": 206, "end": 209, "text": "STS", "label": "TASK"}, {"entityId": "e9", "start": 220, "end": 237, "text": "transfer learning", "label": "TASK"}], "entityStatus": "complete", "entityReason": ""}
